# Correlation and covariance

### Overview

This week we are going to continue looking at the Lenton rainfall data from last week. In the first section you will download daily rainfall data from a *second* location in the UK, compute sample covariance and correlation, and consider whether there is evidence of non-zero correlation.  Then we will consider the distribution of data from a single location and use whether it is consistent with some common continuous distributions. 

## Correlation and covariance of daily rainfall in two locations

Hopefully you have a .csv file with the Lenton data from last week, otherwise please follow instructions from the Week 4 workshop to download that now. 

* Navigate to https://nrfa.ceh.ac.uk/data/search and download data from a *second* location in the UK that is of interest to you. 

```{r data-load, echo = TRUE, results = 'hide',warning=FALSE}
# library used to read csv files
library(readr,warn.conflicts=F, quietly=T)
# library for manipulating data frames
library(dplyr, warn.conflicts=F, quietly=T)


lenton.rainfall <- read_csv("28035_cdr-lenton.csv", col_names = c('date', 'rf'), skip = 20, show_col_types = FALSE)
cambridge.rainfall <- read_csv("33016-cdr-cambridge.csv",  col_names = c('date', 'rf'), skip = 20, show_col_types = FALSE)

# drop columns we don't need
lenton.rainfall <- lenton.rainfall[ -c(3) ]
cambridge.rainfall <- cambridge.rainfall[ -c(3) ]
```

We want to combine these data into a single data frame, matching the entries across locations for each date, so that we have columns date, Lenton rainfall and Cambridge rainfall. As we are interested in correlations between the two samples, we will also remove lines were data is missing from one location. 

```{r match-data, echo = TRUE}
# merge data
data <- full_join(lenton.rainfall, cambridge.rainfall, by='date')
names(data) <- c('date', 'lenton.rf', 'cambridge.rf')
# remove missing data
data <- data[complete.cases(data), ]
```

As for other worksheets, it is a good idea to first visualise your data. 

```{r exploratory, results = TRUE}
par(mfrow = c(1,3))
hist(data$lenton.rf,breaks = seq(from = 0, to = 65, by = 1), xlab = "Daily rainfall (mm)", main = "Lenton")
hist(data$cambridge.rf, breaks = seq(from = 0, to = 65, by = 1),  xlab = "Daily rainfall (mm)", main = "Cambridge")
plot(data$lenton.rf, data$cambridge.rf,xlab = "Lenton daily rf (mm)", ylab = "Cambridge daily rf (mm)")
```

We see that there are many days with little rainfall but a notable tail in the distribution of daily rainfall indicating some very rainy days. It looks like there may be a some correlation between rainfall in each location, but it is hard to tell how strong this is due to the densely clustered data. 

